{
  "dom_id": "step-q267419",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14229367/assets/a8fc1b09d51bf0d7ac526a917b2341015b787b3098627bedd25e1638ecf041cc.png)\n\nThe point $P(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})$ lies on the unit circle, as shown. What is $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ?$ Do not round your answer.\n\n$\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =$\n{{field-1}}",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14229367/assets/fdddfbe8431756e0e0341ad6576383764eb88519cc0454280ce4d443559d3536.png)\n\nAny point $(x,y)$ on the unit circle is related to the central angle $\\theta $ as follows:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \n$$\n\nWe're given that $x=\\frac{2}{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}$ at the point $P.$ Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta =\\frac{2}{3},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3}.\n$$\n\nKnowing that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we can calculate $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{(\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{3})}{(\\frac{2}{3})}=\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2}\n$$",
